      "location": "Statement",
      "defect": "missing-hypothesis",
      "evidence": "The Statement says “let D⊂C^n be bounded with C^1 boundary” and “z∈D,” but does not require D to be open, while asserting both integrals are well-defined. The current dependency `def-bounded-c-one-domain-boundary-charts-and-outward-normal` defines a bounded C^1 domain as a “nonempty bounded open set,” and the cited Stokes result requires such a domain. Literally take n=1, D={|ζ|≤1}, f≡1, and z=1. This bounded set has smooth boundary and z∈D, but the in-scope kernel definition gives Ω1(ζ,1)=dζ/(2πi(ζ−1)); its boundary integral is not absolutely integrable at ζ=1 (under ζ=e^{it}, the integrand magnitude is comparable to 1/|t|). Thus the stated well-definedness fails. Requiring D to be open with z interior excludes this case.",
